= i
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

In **<selective-area epitaxy>**, precursors supplied by <metal-organic vapour-phase epitaxy> incorporate on exposed crystalline InP through patterned openings in a silicon-nitride mask. Epitaxial facet-dependent incorporation and surface <diffusion> select axial and radial <growth rates>. Advantages include lithographic position and pitch control, an epitaxial orientation relationship and the absence of a foreign metal <catalyst>. Limitations include mask patterning, selective <nucleation> requirements, facet-dependent composition, loading and pitch effects, and unwanted radial growth.

In **<vapour-liquid-solid growth>**, precursors decompose and supply material to a liquid <catalyst> droplet; <supersaturation> drives precipitation at the liquid-solid interface. Droplet size can control wire diameter and enable high <aspect ratios>. Limitations include possible <catalyst> contamination, droplet stability and coarsening, composition memory at heterointerfaces, and restrictions from the <catalyst> <phase diagram>. Patterned <catalyst> placement can also control position, so positional ordering is not exclusive to selective-area growth. Conversely, selective-area wires are not automatically free of taper or compositional interfaces.<Efficient Green Emission from Wurtzite AlInP Nanowires by L. Gagliano and others>[Selective-area growth and composition-dependent <nanowire> structure]{ref}

= ii
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The emission energies are approximately
$$
E_{\rm core}=\frac{1240}{615}=2.02\,\mathrm{eV},\qquad E_{\rm shell}=\frac{1240}{690}=1.80\,\mathrm{eV}.
$$
Draw the Al-rich interface as a wider-gap barrier between these two emitting regions: a raised conduction-band edge impedes <electron> transfer, and a lowered valence-band edge impedes <hole> transfer. The following is a consistent straddling-barrier schematic, with the shell having the smaller optical gap.

\Image[paper-2-radial-bands.png]
{title=One consistent core–Al-rich-interface–shell alignment; absolute offsets are not fixed by the emission wavelengths}
{source=paper-2-radial-bands.py}

**The two emission wavelengths determine optical transition energies, not unique conduction- and valence-band offsets.** <Exciton> binding, confinement and <strain> can shift emission relative to the bulk <band gap>. The stated barrier supports inhibited <charge exchange>; its separate <electron> and <hole> barrier heights need additional band-alignment data.

= iii
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Place the Ag particles **near the emitting shell**, along the outer sidewalls or accessible shell at the base. Its $690\,\mathrm{nm}$ emission is close to the $680\,\mathrm{nm}$ <localized surface plasmon resonance>; the $615\,\mathrm{nm}$ core emission is more detuned and farther from an externally placed particle. The plasmon near field decays with distance, so the local emitter-field overlap matters as well as spectral overlap. A thin spacer can reduce direct-contact nonradiative quenching.

These facts identify a favourable placement for enhanced interaction; they do not by themselves prove the spectroscopic **strong-coupling regime**, which additionally requires coherent coupling to exceed the relevant losses and normally produces resolvable mode splitting.

